This chapter covers all the results generated from the project. The results are presented using tables showing the raw values and bar charts for easier visual comparisons. As described in @mth:ev, the main evaluation metrics shown are coherence and diversity. Additionally runtime is also shown.

@res:ind cover the models individually, comparing them to themselves and highlighting under which circumstances they perform best. The subsections also contain some examples of topics generated by the models.

@res:sum summarizes the models together and compare them to each other. This gives a clearer insight as to which model was shown to be the most appropriate for the dataset of Riksarkivet.

== Individual Models <res:ind>

This section details the individual results and evaluated performance of all the tested models and relevant variations. The main focus is on the intrinsic metrics coherence and diversity. Additionally the recorded runtime of the models is also showcased. The main comparisons showcased in this section focuses on comparing the models to themselves under different circumstances. 

For all models this means comparing their performance when tasked to generate different number of topics. For BERTopic and CTM, described in section @res:bertopic and @res:ctm, they are also compared based on the dataset used to generate their dynamic embeddings, the lightly preprocessed dataset described in @mth:d1 as @d1, or the @bow dataset, described in @mth:d2 as @d2. BERTopic specifically is also compared against some variations of itself that uses different settings to change the number of documents classified as "junk" in @res:bertvar. The ETM, described in @res:etm is tested for two different vocabulary sizes. Finally the LDA in @res:lda is only compared based on the number of topics

=== BERTopic <res:bertopic>

As described in the methodology @mth:rp, BERTopic is evaluated on two different versions of the dataset. the versions are described in detail in @mth:data. The first is the lightly preprocessed one that maximizes the semantic structures that BERTopic was designed the take advantage of: @d1. The second version is the @bow representation that removes all semantic context and focuses only on the significant words of the documents: @d2.

BERTopic attempts to generate the requested number of topics, but in order for the topics to be useable for evaluation, they need at least 10 words in them. In the case of BERTopic, some topics fall short of this 10 word requirement. Thus the number of topics presented in @table:bertopicbase represents the target number of topics but the actual number of topics evaluated varies between runs.
