From @fig:bertbasecoher we observe that it achieves its highest coherence score for $K = 30$ topics using the @d2 embeddings. Most notably, the coherence score is consistently higher for the @d2 embeddings compared to when using @d1.

The diversity on the other hand stays more consistent across the two embeddings as well as across different $K$. From @fig:bertbasediv an inverse correlation between diversity and $K$ can be observed, with diversity increasing as the number of topics decrease. The difference is however not major as it only differs with 0.15 between the largest and smallest value

The runtime of BERTopic stays the same independently of $K$ but is consistently lower for embeddings based on @d2 as clearly seen in @fig:bertbasetime.

