Observing the CTM results in @table:ctm and @fig:ctmcoher, we can conclude that the CTM achieves its highest coherence for around 20-40 topics. For both the version using the @d1 and @d2 embeddings, the highest coherence occurred at 20 topics. The difference between using @d1 and @d2 for embeddings is very small as they score very similarly across all topics.


The topic diversity as shown in @fig:ctmdiv, seems to consistently be slightly higher when using @d2. But both embeddings do achieve similarly high values. Visible in both datasets is also a trend of increasing diversity as the number of topics decreases.

We can also note that the runtime for the CTM is much higher than the other models. This is caused by the fact that the CTM is a neural network and the network has to be trained to fit the data every run, which takes significant amount of time. The runtime increases with the amount of topics, differing with around 30 seconds between 10 and 60 topics as seen in @fig:ctmtime. 

The runtime remained very similar between both light and BoW dataset, but was slightly lower when utilizing the BoW dataset for the embeddings when inspecting the values in @table:ctm.

